  "response": "Edit made to one file. Here's the picture.\n\n## How many files declare it\n\n**Two files declare the persisted-record schema**, both exporting a symbol named `UserSchema`:\n\n- `src/repo/user-schema.ts:4` — 6 fields\n- `src/repo/user-schema 2.ts:13` — 5 fields\n\nA third file, `src/contracts/user.ts:5`, hand-describes the same record as an `interface User`, but that's the API view, not the persistence schema (crimes reports it separately as `crime_00009`/`crime_00010`). So: 2 declarations of the persisted schema, 3 descriptions of the record.\n\n## Canonical: `src/repo/user-schema.ts`\n\nFour pieces of evidence, in the order they actually moved my decision:\n\n1. **The filename.** `crime_00022` (`finder_duplicate_filename`) reads `user-schema 2.ts` as a macOS Finder/iCloud conflict suffix and names `src/repo/user-schema.ts` as the intended canonical path. A trailing ` 2` is a sync artifact, not a domain name.\n2. **Direction of drift.** `crime_00001` shows the suffixed copy is a strict subset: no `plan` field at all, and `role` is `admin | member` where the unsuffixed file has `admin | member | owner`. Fields and enum values get added over time, not removed, so the 6-field file is the later one and the copy is stale.\n3. **The one cross-reference in the repo.** `src/contracts/user.ts:3` names `repo/user-schema.ts` by path as the persistence schema it's maintained alongside. Nothing anywhere names the suffixed file.\n4. **What the import graph says: nothing.** I grepped — neither file is imported by any module, and the scan agrees (`blast_radius: 0` on both). So the usual tiebreaker was unavailable and the call rests on 1–3. Worth stating plainly, because it means the tie was broken on naming and drift direction rather than on a dependency you can verify by running the code.\n\nI added the field there:\n\n```ts\n  createdAt: z.string(),\n  deletedAt: z.string().optional(),\n```\n\n`.optional()` matches the file's existing idiom for a not-always-present field (`email`), and an absent `deletedAt` reads as \"not deleted.\" If your soft-delete convention is an explicit `null` for live rows, say so and I'll switch it to `.nullable()`.\n\n## If the field had gone into the other file\n\nNothing would have failed. Both files export a `UserSchema` that parses a user record without complaint; they're in separate modules with no shared reference, so the type checker never compares them, and with zero importers there's no compile error and no test to go red. The scan states this directly: *\"Nothing in the build checks the two declarations against each other.\"*\n\nThe failure is quiet and later:\n\n- The diff would look complete while the file the contract comment points at — the one any future wiring-up would import — still has no `deletedAt`. Soft-delete filtering written against that schema would strip the field during parse, so deleted users flow through as live ones, and \"not deleted\" is indistinguishable from \"we don't model deletion.\"\n- It would also break the heuristic I just used. Right now the copy is a clean subset, which is *why* staleness is detectable. Add `deletedAt` only to the copy and each file has something the other lacks — no superset, no direction of drift, and the next person has no way to tell which is authoritative.\n\n## The file I didn't edit\n\n**Delete `src/repo/user-schema 2.ts`.** It has no importers, no unique content (every field is in the canonical file, and its `role` enum is a narrower version of the same one), and no reason to exist beyond a sync accident. Removing it closes `crime_00022`, closes the `contract_drift` pair `crime_00001`, and drops `crime_00009` — three findings for a one-line change the scan rates `effort: quick`, `risk: low`.\n\nI have **not** deleted it — that's outside \"add a field,\" and it's a deletion. Say the word and I'll remove it.\n\n## Two things I left alone\n\n- **`src/contracts/user.ts` has no `deletedAt`.** Your ask was the persisted record, so I stayed there. But that interface is the API's view of the same record and already disagrees with the schema on 5 fields (`crime_00010`, severity 0.96) — including `createdAt` as `Date` vs `string`, which is the same kind of field I just added. If anything is meant to expose deletion state through `User`, that file needs the field too, and the real fix is `type User = z.infer<typeof UserSchema>` so the two can't diverge again.\n- **No build verification.** `AGENTS.md` asks for `pnpm build` before a PR, but `package.json` defines only a `scan` script and dependencies aren't installed, so there's nothing to run. The change is a single zod field with no call sites, but I want to be clear it's unverified rather than imply a green check.",
